time now for morning rounds with CBS  News chief medical correspondent dr. Jon  LaPook and CBS News contributor dr. Tara  Narula  first up the power of exercise we know  how important it is to our general  health and that's also true when it  comes to pregnancy and an editorial  published this week in the Journal of  the American Medical Association focus  on the importance of exercise for most  pregnant women using the American  College of Obstetricians and  Gynecologists 2015 guidelines the  article highlights a recommendation for  20 to 30 minutes of exercise most days  of the week at a moderate intensity  combining aerobic and strength training  Tero have there been any studies about  how US women are doing it and just how  important is exercise for pregnant women  certain people would like to know so  there was a small study that came out in  2016 that looked at about 250 pregnant  women and asked them about their  physical activity and 60 percent of  those women reported no leisure time  physical activity and those meeting the  guidelines ranged anywhere from about 13  to 45 percent so not doing so great but  I love what the authors of the editorial  said which is really that pregnancy is  no longer considered a state of  confinement that exercise is not only  safe but it is beneficial it increases  your longevity your cardio respiratory  fitness your just overall emotional  well-being and it can decrease things  like gestational diabetes preeclampsia  problems in terms of increased risk of  of c-section so less risk of that in  addition less low back pain less pelvic  problems and less urinary incontinence  so all good things we're not talking  rock climbing here right what kind of  exercise are we first thing they do make  a specific point of saying is you have  to check with your doctor make sure it's  safe for you to do it but they're  talking about things like walking and  dancing stationary bike jogging swimming  the things that you should avoid are  things like very vigorous very ultra  vigorous stuff and high impact say  long-distance running or heavy weight  lifting but I love the fact that they  specifically point out a few other  things they said you should definitely  avoid boxing as you attempted ice hockey  and then my favorite is skydiving and  scuba divers they say  the first thing on my list if not things  not to do well my wife played golf the  day before she gave birth both times so  you know maybe it accelerates things a  little bit that's not on the list though  golf I don't think so it's quite good  but seriously are there are the people  who should who should not be doing this  kind of thing in their pregnancy so  there are certain groups of women who  it's recommended just stick to plain  walking and those are women with sort of  high-grade heart disease or lung disease  women who have severe anemia  pregnancy-induced hypertension or  preeclampsia those with problems meaning  that their increased increased risk of  ruptured membranes they have an  incompetent cervix bleeding in the  second or third trimester or placenta  previa okay okay  our next topic community health centers  a report from the Kaiser Family  Foundation found that these centers  provided care for more than 24 million  patients in 2015 including nearly one in  six individuals on Medicaid how do these  centers might be impacted by any new  health care law is a looming question  dr. LaPook took a closer look  despina every day at the Erie Family  Health Center in Chicago doctor mark  Simon sees patients mostly the working  poor people can come in and get their  cancer screenings their blood pressure  check the cholesterol checked and and  also I think equally important they can  they can have a medical home dr. Simon  has seen his practice dramatically  expand under Obamacare the number of  patients earing now serves has almost  doubled over four years - more than  68,000 63% get medicaid of the 24  million people the Congressional Budget  Office said could have lost their health  insurance under the most recent GOP  proposal Erie Health estimates that  could have included 9,000 of its  patients 61 year old retired taxi driver  Leslie Durham has heart disease and he  could be one of them what kind of  medical care were you getting before you  had the insurance no whatsoever  all I had to do is anytime I feel some  pain  I had to go to Cook County Hospital to  the emergency room I had to wake up at 3  o'clock in the morning to be there early  you want to be the first person and I'll  be the first person in line to get  inside to go to the ER Doron now  volunteers coaching soccer he's  recovered from quadruple bypass surgery  last year and dreads any change that  could leave him without insurance yet  again anything happen I'm gonna die not  only me me me others is this keeping you  up at night yes yes it is I see faces  and see smiles and I feel it that they  might be slipping away you know there  was another man that I meant another  patient when I was in Chicago 60  year-old man born in Greece  salt-of-the-earth worked hard his whole  life he fell on hard times  lost his private insurance had to go on  Medicaid he hated being on Medicaid even  though he had it he didn't go to the  doctor because he felt there was a  stigma attached to it and he told me and  he said I've goose bumps telling you  this because I've been so ashamed I  haven't even told my own kids he said I  was so ashamed to go to a Medicaid  person place and show my a Medicaid card  but now with Obamacare he was able to go  in and show just an insurance card and  he said nobody knew that he was on  Medicaid and nobody thought oh he's  looking for a handout he's poor it's  something I have to confess I really  haven't thought a lot about just yeah  when we have this debate over Medicaid  and health insurance it's important to  remember that there are human beings on  the front line of these stories
 

